data: {"item_id":"rs_931576","output_index":0,"sequence_number":107,"summary_index":0,"text":"Okay, the user is asking why retry policies usually cap the delay between attempts. Let me think. So, retry policies are there to avoid infinite loops when an error occurs. If they didn't cap the delay, the next attempt could be much later, leading to a delay that grows exponentially. That could overwhelm the system. Capping the delay prevents that exponential growth, ensuring the system remains responsive. So the two sentences would explain that capting delays prevents infinite loops and avoids system overload by limiting the time between retries.\n","type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done"}

